[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports held two problems.
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October, and the two problems ended differently.

<Callout kind="warn">
Parser: fixed by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Hint: won't fix, because the booking-v3
form is retired. The parser fix did not touch the hint problem.
</Callout>

## What the reports hold

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Each report counts once, by its id, because the export pages overlap. The cause comes from the
report's tenant and channel, as `causes.ts` runs it. Do not count intake's tag: it is a guess from
the reporter's browser, and the table shows how far it strays.

The table's last column reads the desk's export, which predates the two tickets below and the
engineering update.

## The two problems

**The shared parser read dates month first.** A tenant that writes the day first got the day and
month swapped. This is what #4471 describes. Engineering fixed it. Ticket:
[parser](https://github.com/jasondashaer/mega-dev-challenges/issues/4), closed as completed.

**The booking-v3 form printed the wrong hint.** Its date field said `MM/DD/YYYY`, so people typed
the month first, and the form read the date the tenant's way, day first. These reports came through
that form from a day-first tenant. Engineering will not fix it, because the form is retired. Ticket:
[hint](https://github.com/jasondashaer/mega-dev-challenges/issues/5), closed as not planned.

Both looked the same in a report: a date with its day and month swapped. So the body does not tell
them apart. The way the report came in and the form the tenant runs do.

## What is still unproven

The one-day reports in the note under the table are counted where they are filed. A swap of day and
month does not explain a one-day move, so the parser fix may not have ended them. If they continue,
open a new ticket.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line. The two problems
share subject lines: every subject on a hint report also appears on parser reports. So the rule
could not keep them apart. The decision stays as written; a new record has to replace it.

